#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Access-control (IDOR/BOLA/BFLA) testing via multi-identity replay.

The #1 web/API risk and the one scanners can't find — because it needs ground
truth only the operator has: who legitimately owns what. You declare identities
(auth by env-var reference) and concrete tests (a request + the owner identity);
this replays each request as every identity and lets the response diff deliver
the verdict. A non-owner reading the owner's object is a confirmed break, proven
by the diff (not an LLM), and surfaced as a verified outcome.

Two modes:
  --init   seed an authz-config.json from an api-inventory.json / authz-matrix.json
  (default) run the tests from --config

SAFE BY DEFAULT: no request is sent unless --active is passed AND the config
declares a non-empty `authorization` attestation (the mechanical gate). Without
--active you get a dry-run plan + the surface graph.

Usage:
  raptor-webauthz --out-dir <dir> --init --inventory api-inventory.json
  raptor-webauthz --out-dir <dir> --config authz-config.json            (dry-run)
  raptor-webauthz --out-dir <dir> --config authz-config.json --active   (sends traffic)

Output (under --out-dir):
  authz-config.json (--init) | webauthz-findings.json | graph/web.* | verified outcomes
Exit codes: 0 ok, 2 usage / gate error.
"""
import sys
from pathlib import Path

sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]))

from core.webauthz.cli import main  # noqa: E402

if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
